  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A rise in real output. Improving welfare is development, a rise in prices is inflation, and population growth is a change in the size of the economy's base, not in its output.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a wider concept. Growth is one number; development is a judged condition that includes health, schooling, employment and inequality.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Growth without development: output rose, but welfare did not improve on two standard measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Income, health and knowledge. The other options mix in variables the index does not use.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The income has not translated into health or schooling — the classic case of growth without development, often with severe inequality.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The population. A denominator of the labour force would give income per worker, a productivity figure rather than a welfare one.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dependants over the working-age population. The second option inverts it, the third is the participation rate, and the fourth is the unemployment rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dependants are 30 + 10 = 40 million; 40 ÷ 60 × 100 = 66.7 per cent. 50 per cent counts only the children, and 150 per cent inverts the ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each worker supports more non-workers, so saving and investment are harder and public spending on schools and health is higher.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A broad base means many children, which implies high dependency and a labour force that will grow quickly as those children reach working age.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Employed plus actively seeking. Full-time students, retirees and discouraged workers are outside it, and counting them would distort the unemployment rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Labour force over working-age population. The second is the employment rate and the third is the unemployment rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    4 ÷ (36 + 4) × 100 = 10 per cent. 4 per cent divides by the employed only, 11.1 per cent divides by the employed and then adds, and 4.4 per cent uses the wrong base again.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Frictional — the short search period in any working labour market. It is not caused by a skill mismatch or by the state of demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Structural: the skill or industry no longer exists where it is needed, so the cure is retraining and redeployment rather than stimulus.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cyclical, also called demand-deficient. The cure is reflation, which is why the cause matters — the same symptom has different remedies.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Seasonal, because the work follows a calendar. The cure is diversification and planning, not demand stimulus.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Retraining, because the problem is a mismatch of skills. More demand would not create jobs for skills that no longer exist.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reflation. Demand is deficient, so the cure is to raise demand — the other options address different kinds of unemployment.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It counts as employment in the official figures even though the worker's capacity is not fully used, which is why the unemployment rate can understate the problem.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Discouraged workers stop looking, so they leave the labour force and stop being counted as unemployed — the participation rate is the figure that reveals it.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    High-to-low on both rates, with the death rate falling first — which is why population grows fastest in the middle of the transition.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Per capita income falls, because the same or slower output is shared among more people. This is the arithmetic behind the population trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Output grows more slowly than the number of people, so income per head falls by roughly the difference — about 1 per cent a year.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Gini coefficient, which runs from 0 for perfect equality to 1 for complete inequality. The others measure welfare, prices and age structure respectively.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A fixed standard — a poverty line. Relative poverty, by contrast, is defined against the living standards of the society concerned.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Human capital: the skills and health embodied in people, which raise their productivity and the economy's output.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The formal sector absorbs only part of the labour force, so the rest creates its own work outside registration and taxation.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The expected wage gap and the services, even though the actual urban outcome for many migrants is informal work and poor housing.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two obligations run in sequence, and failing the second turns a demographic dividend into a demographic burden.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Literacy is social. GDP, per capita income and the balance of payments are all monetary or output measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Coordination and guidance. A plan in a mixed economy sets direction and public investment; it does not replace markets or fix every price.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Growth in output is swallowed by growth in numbers, so income per head never rises. It is the arithmetic of the previous question generalised over time.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Agriculture dominates employment, productivity is low, the informal sector is large and population growth is usually rapid.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    No demand-deficient unemployment. Some frictional and structural unemployment is unavoidable, so a zero rate is not the target.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    High capital per worker raises productivity rather than causing unemployment; the other three are the standard causes.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It matches the technology to the endowment: with abundant labour, labour-intensive methods create more jobs per naira invested.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Between 0 and 1, as a geometric mean of the three scaled components. The index is often reported to three decimal places.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Health and schooling improvements raise two of the three components directly, and the index can rise without any change in income.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Phillips-curve trade-off holds in the short run but shifts when expectations change, which is why it is a menu rather than a law.
  ]

]

